/* AbiLearn — Social Science MCQ Bank — Geography Ch 14 & Ch 15 (100 Questions) */

// CH14_SOCIAL — Forest and Wildlife Resources
const SOCIAL_CH14 = [
  { q: 'India holds approximately what percentage of the world\'s total biodiversity despite occupying only 2.4% of the world\'s land area?', opts: ['4%', '6%', '8%', '12%'], ans: 2, exp: 'India accounts for about 8% of the world\'s total biodiversity. This remarkable figure — achieved despite the country covering only 2.4% of global land — places India among the 17 mega-diverse nations. It harbours around 47,000 plant species and 89,000 animal species.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following correctly states India\'s biodiversity figures as given in NCERT Class 10 Geography?', opts: ['47,000 plant species, 75,000 animal species, 1,000 bird species', '52,000 plant species, 89,000 animal species, 1,200 bird species', '47,000 plant species, 89,000 animal species, 1,200 bird species', '40,000 plant species, 89,000 animal species, 900 bird species'], ans: 2, exp: 'India has about 47,000 plant species (of which 15,000 are flowering plants), 89,000 animal species (including 2,000 species of birds and 2,546 species of fish), and around 1,200 species of birds. These statistics underscore India\'s status as one of the world\'s most biodiverse nations.' },
  { q: 'Out of India\'s approximately 47,000 plant species, roughly how many are flowering plants?', opts: ['5,000', '10,000', '15,000', '25,000'], ans: 2, exp: 'Of India\'s 47,000 plant species, approximately 15,000 are flowering plants. India\'s plant diversity ranges from alpine species in the Himalayas to tropical species in the Western Ghats and Andaman & Nicobar Islands, representing a significant share of global plant biodiversity.' },
  { q: 'Under the IUCN Red List classification, the Asiatic elephant is placed in which category?', opts: ['Endangered', 'Vulnerable', 'Normal/Least Concern', 'Endemic'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Asiatic elephant is categorised as Vulnerable on the IUCN Red List. Vulnerable species face a high risk of extinction in the wild if the circumstances that are threatening their survival and reproduction continue. The Asiatic elephant is distinct from the Endangered Asian elephant sub-populations.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following sets of animals is correctly classified as "Endangered" under the IUCN Red List as mentioned in the Class 10 NCERT?', opts: ['Black buck, Indian crocodile, snow leopard', 'Blue sheep, Gangetic dolphin, hornbill', 'Himalayan brown bear, desert fox, Nicobar pigeon', 'Andaman teal, mountain quail, pink-headed duck'], ans: 0, exp: 'The NCERT classifies black buck, crocodile, Indian wild ass, Indian rhinoceros, snow leopard, and lion as Endangered. Endangered species face a very high risk of extinction in the wild in the near future. Blue sheep and Gangetic dolphin are Vulnerable; the others listed are in different categories.' },
  { q: 'The Gangetic dolphin (Platanista gangetica) is listed under which IUCN Red List category as per Class 10 NCERT Geography?', opts: ['Endangered', 'Vulnerable', 'Rare', 'Normal'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Gangetic dolphin is listed as Vulnerable. It is one of the world\'s few river dolphins and is found in the Ganga-Brahmaputra-Meghna and Karnaphuli-Sangu river systems. Habitat degradation, pollution, accidental killing in fishing nets, and direct hunting have made it vulnerable to extinction.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following animals falls under the "Rare" category in the IUCN Red List classification as given in the NCERT?', opts: ['Snow leopard', 'Gangetic dolphin', 'Himalayan brown bear', 'Indian wild ass'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Himalayan brown bear, desert fox, and hornbill are classified as Rare species — species with small populations that are not currently Endangered or Vulnerable but face risk. Rare species may become threatened if conditions worsen. Snow leopard and Indian wild ass are Endangered.' },
  { q: 'The Andaman teal and the Nicobar pigeon are examples of which IUCN Red List category?', opts: ['Vulnerable', 'Endangered', 'Rare', 'Endemic'], ans: 3, exp: 'The Andaman teal, Nicobar pigeon, and Andaman wild pig are classified as Endemic species — organisms found only in a specific geographic area and nowhere else in the world. The Andaman and Nicobar Islands\' geographic isolation has led to high endemism. Note that "Endemic" as used here is an NCERT categorical classification.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following animals is classified as "Extinct" under the IUCN categories as listed in Class 10 NCERT?', opts: ['Mountain quail, forest spotted owlet, pink-headed duck', 'Himalayan brown bear, desert fox, hornbill', 'Indian wild ass, snow leopard, lion', 'Andaman teal, Nicobar pigeon, Gangetic dolphin'], ans: 0, exp: 'The pink-headed duck (Rhodonessa caryophyllacea), mountain quail (Ophrysia superciliosa), and forest spotted owlet (Athene blewitti) are believed to be extinct or possibly extinct. The pink-headed duck was last reliably seen in 1935. Extinction means no known individual of the species is alive anywhere.' },
  { q: 'Project Tiger was launched in India in which year, and with how many tiger reserves initially?', opts: ['1970, with 5 reserves', '1973, with 9 reserves', '1975, with 12 reserves', '1980, with 7 reserves'], ans: 1, exp: 'Project Tiger was launched on 1 April 1973 with 9 tiger reserves covering an area of about 16,339 sq km. Jim Corbett National Park in Uttarakhand was the first reserve. The project was a response to the drastic decline in tiger numbers from an estimated 40,000 at the turn of the century to fewer than 1,827 in 1972.' },
  { q: 'India\'s first National Park was established in which year and in which state?', opts: ['Jim Corbett National Park, Uttarakhand, 1936', 'Bharatpur Bird Sanctuary, Rajasthan, 1956', 'Kaziranga National Park, Assam, 1908', 'Sariska Wildlife Sanctuary, Rajasthan, 1955'], ans: 0, exp: 'Jim Corbett National Park in Uttarakhand (formerly United Provinces, Hailey National Park) was established in 1936, making it India\'s first national park. It was later renamed in honour of the hunter-naturalist Jim Corbett who championed its creation. It was also among the first reserves under Project Tiger 1973.' },
  { q: 'The Nilgiris Biosphere Reserve holds the distinction of being:', opts: ['India\'s largest biosphere reserve by area', 'India\'s first biosphere reserve, established in 1986', 'The biosphere reserve with the highest tiger population', 'The first UNESCO World Heritage site in peninsular India'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Nilgiris Biosphere Reserve in Tamil Nadu/Kerala/Karnataka was India\'s first biosphere reserve, established in 1986. It was also the first Indian biosphere reserve to be included in the UNESCO World Network of Biosphere Reserves (2000). Biosphere reserves are meant to protect biodiversity while allowing sustainable use.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is NOT listed as a Biosphere Reserve in the Class 10 NCERT chapter on Forest and Wildlife Resources?', opts: ['Sundarbans', 'Nanda Devi', 'Gulf of Mannar', 'Keoladeo Ghana'], ans: 3, exp: 'Keoladeo Ghana (Bharatpur Bird Sanctuary, Rajasthan) is a National Park and Ramsar Wetland, not a Biosphere Reserve. The NCERT mentions Sundarbans, Nanda Devi, Gulf of Mannar, Manas, and Nilgiris as key Biosphere Reserves. Biosphere Reserves are categorised into core, buffer, and transition zones.' },
  { q: 'The Joint Forest Management (JFM) programme in India was formally initiated through a government resolution in which year?', opts: ['1972', '1988', '1990', '2002'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Government of India formally initiated the Joint Forest Management (JFM) programme in 1990 through a resolution, encouraging state forest departments to work with local communities. Under JFM, Village Forest Committees (VFCs) manage degraded forests and share the benefits. By 2002, over 63,000 JFM committees managed about 14 million hectares.' },
  { q: 'The Arabari experiment, a pioneering model of community forest management, was conducted in which state and involved which type of forest?', opts: ['Maharashtra — Teak forests', 'West Bengal — Sal forests', 'Jharkhand — Bamboo forests', 'Odisha — Mangrove forests'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Arabari experiment was conducted in 1972 in Arabari in Midnapore district of West Bengal. Forest officer A.K. Banerjee involved local communities in protecting degraded Sal (Shorea robusta) forests. Within ten years, the nearly worthless Sal scrub was transformed into productive forest worth ₹12.5 crore, vindicating participatory forest management.' },
  { q: 'The Chipko Movement, a landmark environmental protest to save forests, originated in which year and region?', opts: ['1970, in Kerala\'s Silent Valley', '1973, in Chamoli/Gopeshwar, Uttarakhand', '1974, in Rajasthan\'s Aravalli hills', '1982, in Karnataka\'s Western Ghats'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Chipko Movement began in 1973 in Gopeshwar (Chamoli district), Uttarakhand. Women led by Gaura Devi in Reni village (1974) and activists Chandi Prasad Bhatt and Sundarlal Bahuguna were key figures. Protesters hugged (chipko = to cling/hug) trees to prevent felling, eventually contributing to a 15-year ban on green felling in Himalayan forests.' },
  { q: 'Gaura Devi is associated with which forest conservation movement, and for what specific act?', opts: ['Navdanya movement — saving indigenous seeds in Uttarakhand', 'Chipko Movement — leading women to hug trees at Reni village in 1974', 'Beej Bachao Andolan — preventing sale of forests to contractors', 'Silent Valley protest — blocking a hydroelectric project in Kerala'], ans: 1, exp: 'Gaura Devi was a village woman from Reni in Chamoli district, Uttarakhand. In March 1974, she led a group of women to confront forest fellers sent by a sports goods company. The women hugged the trees, preventing their felling. This became one of the most iconic acts of the Chipko Movement and highlighted women\'s role in environmental protection.' },
  { q: 'The Beej Bachao Andolan (Save the Seeds Movement) is associated with which region and what primary objective?', opts: ['Rajasthan — conserving water bodies and traditional ponds', 'Uttarakhand/Tehri-Garhwal — preserving traditional seed varieties and biodiversity', 'West Bengal — protecting mangrove forests from shrimp farming', 'Tamil Nadu — saving native rice varieties from corporate patents'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Beej Bachao Andolan was started in the Tehri-Garhwal region of Uttarakhand. Activists like Vijay Jardhari collected and preserved hundreds of traditional crop varieties (seeds) that were being displaced by Green Revolution monocultures. The related Navdanya movement, led by Dr. Vandana Shiva, also works on seed sovereignty and biodiversity conservation.' },
  { q: 'Sacred groves ("Dev vans" or "Van" in many parts of India) serve primarily which ecological function?', opts: ['They are government-protected zones exclusively for research purposes', 'They are community-protected forest patches where all flora and fauna are inviolable, preserving biodiversity', 'They are areas where only medicinal plants are cultivated under religious supervision', 'They are forest areas allocated exclusively for tribal communities\' timber needs'], ans: 1, exp: 'Sacred groves are forest patches protected by local communities through religious and cultural taboos. No tree, plant, or animal can be harmed within them. They are found across India — "sarnas" or "jaher" in Jharkhand (Sarna faith communities), "devsai" in Maharashtra, "Dev vans" in Himachal Pradesh. They function as biodiversity refugia, often preserving rare species.' },
  { q: 'In Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh, sacred groves are known by which name?', opts: ['Dev vans', 'Devsai', 'Sarnas or Jaher', 'Kavu'], ans: 2, exp: 'In Jharkhand (and among tribal communities in Chhattisgarh), sacred groves are called "sarnas" or "jaher." These are protected under the belief that local deities reside in these forests. They may not be cleared or harvested. Similar traditions exist across India — "kavu" in Kerala, "devsai" in Maharashtra, "Dev vans" in Himachal Pradesh.' },
  { q: 'The Forest Rights Act (Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers Recognition of Forest Rights Act) was passed in which year?', opts: ['1996', '2002', '2006', '2010'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Forest Rights Act (FRA) was passed in 2006 and came into force in 2008. It recognised the rights of Scheduled Tribes and other traditional forest dwellers who had been living in forests for generations but lacked legal titles. It granted individual and community rights over forest land and resources, correcting historical injustices.' },
  { q: 'Under Project Tiger, the number of tiger reserves has expanded significantly from its initial 9 in 1973. By the time the NCERT textbook was updated, how many tiger reserves were there in India?', opts: ['More than 25', 'More than 50', 'Exactly 30', 'More than 100'], ans: 1, exp: 'From the original 9 tiger reserves in 1973, Project Tiger expanded to over 50 tiger reserves across India (the exact figure continues to grow; as of 2023 there are 53). Project Tiger is widely credited with saving the tiger from imminent extinction — tiger numbers increased from under 1,827 in 1972 to over 3,000 in the 2022 census.' },
  { q: 'Project Elephant, aimed at protecting free-ranging elephant populations and their habitats, was launched in which year?', opts: ['1985', '1992', '1998', '2003'], ans: 1, exp: 'Project Elephant was launched in 1992 by the Government of India to protect elephants, their habitats, and migration corridors. It also addresses human-elephant conflict. The project provides financial and technical support to states with free-ranging elephant populations, primarily in Assam, Kerala, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, and Uttarakhand.' },
  { q: 'The term "biosphere reserve" differs from a "national park" primarily in that biosphere reserves:', opts: ['Are exclusively for endangered species and ban all human activity', 'Allow sustainable human use in buffer/transition zones while protecting a core zone', 'Are managed by the IUCN rather than national governments', 'Are smaller in area than national parks but more strictly protected'], ans: 1, exp: 'Biosphere reserves have a three-zone structure: (1) Core zone — strictly protected, no human activity; (2) Buffer zone — limited research and eco-tourism; (3) Transition/manipulation zone — sustainable human activities allowed. National parks are areas where no human activity except wildlife protection is permitted. Biosphere reserves integrate conservation with sustainable development.' },
  { q: 'The Sundarbans Biosphere Reserve in West Bengal is particularly significant for protecting which endangered species?', opts: ['Indian one-horned rhinoceros', 'Royal Bengal tiger and Irrawaddy dolphin', 'Hoolock gibbon and clouded leopard', 'Indian wild ass and black buck'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Sundarbans — the world\'s largest mangrove delta — is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and Biosphere Reserve. It is the last refuge of the Royal Bengal tiger (a major Project Tiger reserve) and also hosts the Irrawaddy dolphin, saltwater crocodile, and the endangered river terrapin. The mangrove ecosystem supports unique biodiversity.' },
  { q: 'The Bharatpur Bird Sanctuary (Keoladeo Ghana National Park) in Rajasthan is famous for being a wintering ground for which critically important migratory birds?', opts: ['Flamingos from East Africa', 'Siberian cranes from Central Asia and Siberia', 'Arctic terns from the polar regions', 'Bar-headed geese from China'], ans: 1, exp: 'Bharatpur Bird Sanctuary (Keoladeo Ghana National Park) was famous for hosting the Siberian crane (Grus leucogeranus), one of the world\'s rarest birds, during winter. However, Siberian cranes stopped visiting after 2002. It remains a major site for thousands of migratory waterfowl from Central Asia, Siberia, and Europe.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following correctly describes the difference between a Wildlife Sanctuary and a National Park in India?', opts: ['National Parks allow regulated grazing and human habitation; Wildlife Sanctuaries do not', 'Wildlife Sanctuaries allow limited human activities like grazing; National Parks prohibit all human activities', 'Both are identical in management but differ only in size', 'Wildlife Sanctuaries are managed by the Centre; National Parks are managed by States'], ans: 1, exp: 'Wildlife Sanctuaries permit certain human activities such as regulated grazing, collection of non-timber forest produce (NTFP), and private ownership of land within boundaries. National Parks have stricter protection — no human habitation, grazing, or forestry is permitted. Both are protected areas under the Wildlife Protection Act, 1972.' },
  { q: 'The destruction of forest habitats in India has been accelerated by which of the following human activities? Choose the most comprehensive answer.', opts: ['Only timber poaching by tribal communities', 'Agricultural expansion, large development projects, mining, urbanisation, and commercial logging', 'Overgrazing by wild animals causing forest degradation', 'Solely climate change reducing rainfall in forested areas'], ans: 1, exp: 'Deforestation in India results from multiple interacting causes: expansion of agriculture (post-independence, large tracts were cleared); large development projects like dams and roads; mining and quarrying; urbanisation; commercial timber exploitation; and fuel wood collection. Between 1951 and 1980, over 26,200 sq km of forest was cleared for development projects alone.' },
  { q: 'The practice of shifting cultivation (Jhum or swidden farming) contributes to deforestation by:', opts: ['Permanently increasing soil fertility in cleared areas', 'Permanently converting forest to farmland as fallow periods have shortened under population pressure', 'Maintaining forest cover because plots are abandoned and regenerate after one season', 'Planting nitrogen-fixing trees on cleared plots, enriching biodiversity'], ans: 1, exp: 'Traditional shifting cultivation with long fallow periods (15–20 years) allowed forest regeneration. However, population pressure has shortened fallow periods to 2–3 years in many areas, preventing adequate forest recovery. The practice thus contributes to permanent forest degradation rather than a sustainable cycle, particularly in northeast India.' },
  { q: 'The Dudhwa National Park and Tiger Reserve is located in which state?', opts: ['Uttarakhand', 'Uttar Pradesh', 'Assam', 'West Bengal'], ans: 1, exp: 'Dudhwa National Park is located in Lakhimpur Kheri district of Uttar Pradesh, along the India-Nepal border in the Terai region. It was established as a National Park in 1977 and became a Tiger Reserve under Project Tiger. It hosts tigers, rhinoceroses (reintroduced), swamp deer (barasingha), and elephants.' },
  { q: 'The Rajaji National Park, known for its elephant population and established after merging three wildlife sanctuaries, is located in which state?', opts: ['Rajasthan', 'Madhya Pradesh', 'Uttarakhand', 'Himachal Pradesh'], ans: 2, exp: 'Rajaji National Park is located in the Shivalik foothills of Uttarakhand (Haridwar, Pauri Garhwal, Dehradun districts). It was established in 1983 by merging Rajaji, Motichur, and Chilla sanctuaries. It is one of the largest protected areas in Uttarakhand and is a major elephant habitat in the western Himalayan foothills.' },
  { q: 'The Manas Biosphere Reserve, a UNESCO World Heritage Site and Project Tiger reserve, is located in which state and is particularly known for which species?', opts: ['West Bengal — Royal Bengal Tiger', 'Assam — Golden Langur and Pygmy Hog', 'Meghalaya — Clouded Leopard', 'Arunachal Pradesh — Snow Leopard'], ans: 1, exp: 'Manas Biosphere Reserve and Tiger Reserve is located in Assam, on the foothills of Bhutan. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of India\'s most biodiverse areas. It is particularly known for the golden langur (Trachypithecus geei), pygmy hog (Porcula salvania — world\'s smallest pig), and hispid hare — all highly endangered species.' },
  { q: 'The Gulf of Mannar Biosphere Reserve is significant for which unique ecological feature?', opts: ['It contains the world\'s largest freshwater mangrove ecosystem', 'It contains India\'s only coral reef ecosystems along with seagrass beds and sea cucumber colonies', 'It is the only biosphere reserve shared between India and Sri Lanka', 'It is India\'s largest marine biosphere reserve with whale shark populations'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Gulf of Mannar Biosphere Reserve (established 1989) stretches between Rameswaram and Thoothukudi in Tamil Nadu. It contains some of India\'s most extensive coral reefs, seagrass meadows, and mangroves. It is also a critical habitat for the endangered dugong (sea cow). It was the first Marine Biosphere Reserve in South and Southeast Asia.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best explains why India\'s forest cover decline has had outsized ecological consequences compared to other countries losing similar acreage?', opts: ['India\'s forests have lower biodiversity than tropical forests elsewhere', 'India\'s forests are concentrated in biodiversity hotspots — Western Ghats and North-East — making even small losses ecologically devastating', 'India\'s timber forests are economically less valuable than those of Brazil or Indonesia', 'India\'s forests cover more than 30% of land, so losses are proportionally less significant'], ans: 1, exp: 'India contains two global biodiversity hotspots: the Western Ghats and the Eastern Himalaya (which includes Northeast India). These areas have extraordinarily high endemism and species density. Forest loss in these hotspots causes disproportionate extinction of endemic species found nowhere else. India\'s forest cover (~21%) is also below the ideal 33% recommended in the National Forest Policy 1988.' },
  { q: 'The National Forest Policy 1988 recommended that what percentage of India\'s geographical area should be under forest and tree cover?', opts: ['20%', '25%', '33%', '40%'], ans: 2, exp: 'The National Forest Policy of 1988 recommended maintaining 33% of India\'s total geographical area under forest and tree cover (67% for hilly/tribal areas and 20% for plains). India\'s actual forest cover is approximately 21%, significantly below this target. The gap between policy and reality reflects ongoing pressures from development, agriculture, and illegal felling.' },
  { q: 'The pink-headed duck (Rhodonessa caryophyllacea) was last reliably sighted in approximately which year, leading to its classification as extinct?', opts: ['1910', '1935', '1952', '1975'], ans: 1, exp: 'The pink-headed duck, once found in the wetlands of Bihar, Bengal, and Assam, was last reliably seen in 1935. Despite occasional unverified reports, no confirmed sighting has occurred since, leading to its classification as Extinct. Its disappearance is attributed to habitat loss (wetland drainage), overhunting, and possibly disease.' },
  { q: 'Which specific statement about India\'s fish biodiversity is correct?', opts: ['India has about 1,000 species of freshwater fish', 'India accounts for about 2,546 species of fish — nearly 12% of the world\'s species', 'India has no significant marine fish biodiversity', 'India has 500 species of fish, all freshwater'], ans: 1, exp: 'India harbours approximately 2,546 species of fish, representing about 12% of the world\'s fish species — a remarkable proportion for a country of India\'s size. This includes both freshwater and marine species. The Western Ghats rivers and Himalayan rivers are particularly rich in endemic freshwater fish species.' },
  { q: 'The IUCN Red List category "Vulnerable" means a species:', opts: ['Has already gone extinct in the wild', 'Faces an extremely high risk of extinction in the immediate future', 'Faces a high risk of extinction in the medium-term future if threatening factors continue', 'Has a small population but is not currently at risk of extinction'], ans: 2, exp: 'A Vulnerable species faces a high risk of extinction in the wild in the medium-term future (roughly 10 years or three generations) if the factors causing its decline continue. It is less critical than Endangered (very high risk) or Critically Endangered (extremely high risk). Blue sheep and Asiatic elephant are listed as Vulnerable in the NCERT.' },
  { q: 'The Nanda Devi Biosphere Reserve in Uttarakhand is significant for hosting which globally important ecosystem type?', opts: ['Tropical rainforest', 'Alpine meadows and glacial ecosystems of the high Himalayas', 'Semi-arid scrubland', 'Mangrove delta systems'], ans: 1, exp: 'Nanda Devi Biosphere Reserve (established 1988), centred on Nanda Devi Peak (7,816 m — India\'s second highest), encompasses alpine meadows (bugyals), glaciers, moraines, and subalpine forests. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. It harbours snow leopard, Himalayan brown bear, serow, and bharal (blue sheep) — several of them Endangered or Vulnerable.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is the most accurate reason why the extinction of a "keystone species" — like the tiger — would have cascading ecological consequences?', opts: ['Tigers are culturally important, so their loss affects only human psychology', 'Tigers regulate herbivore populations; their loss causes vegetation collapse through overgrazing and ecosystem degradation', 'Tigers produce nutrients that directly fertilise forests', 'The loss of tigers only affects the deer population, with no wider consequences'], ans: 1, exp: 'Keystone species like tigers maintain ecosystem balance by regulating prey populations (deer, wild boar). Without tigers, herbivore populations would explode, causing overgrazing and vegetation depletion. This would trigger soil erosion, stream sedimentation, reduced water retention, and cascading losses of other species — demonstrating the interconnected "web of life" that the chapter discusses.' },
  { q: 'The Wildlife Protection Act of India was enacted in which year, providing the legal framework for protecting species and establishing protected areas?', opts: ['1960', '1972', '1980', '1988'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Wildlife (Protection) Act was enacted in 1972. It provides for the protection of wildlife species and habitats, establishes the legal categories of National Parks, Wildlife Sanctuaries, and Conservation Reserves, and creates schedules listing protected species. Hunting and trade of Schedule I species is completely banned. Project Tiger was also established under this act.' },
  { q: 'The Chipko activist Sundarlal Bahuguna\'s contribution to forest conservation extended beyond Chipko to which other major campaign?', opts: ['Opposing the Tehri Dam project on ecological grounds', 'Opposing the Silent Valley hydroelectric project in Kerala', 'Leading the Narmada Bachao Andolan against the Sardar Sarovar Dam', 'Founding the Navdanya seed conservation network'], ans: 0, exp: 'Sundarlal Bahuguna, a key Chipko activist, also led a major campaign against the Tehri Dam on the Bhagirathi River in Uttarakhand, citing earthquake risks (the dam lies in seismic zone IV/V), submergence of forests and villages, and damage to the Himalayan ecosystem. He undertook multiple hunger strikes against the project. The dam was eventually completed in 2006.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "community conserved areas" (sacred groves, etc.) is important because it demonstrates that:', opts: ['Indigenous communities have always been the primary cause of deforestation', 'Local communities, given rights and incentives, can protect biodiversity more effectively than the state alone', 'Sacred groves are only found in South Asia and have no parallel elsewhere', 'Conservation requires strict exclusion of all human activities with no exceptions'], ans: 1, exp: 'Sacred groves and community conserved areas demonstrate the effectiveness of community-based conservation rooted in local culture, religion, and economic self-interest. They predate modern conservation by centuries. Studies show many sacred groves harbour species not found in adjacent reserved forests. This validates the JFM approach and challenges the exclusionary "fortress conservation" model.' },
  { q: 'The hornbill, classified as "Rare" by NCERT, is particularly significant ecologically because:', opts: ['It is a carnivorous bird that controls rodent populations in forests', 'It is a keystone seed disperser — distributing large-seeded trees across long distances, critical for forest regeneration', 'It produces materials used in traditional medicine that no other species can provide', 'It controls insect populations that would otherwise destroy forest canopy'], ans: 1, exp: 'Hornbills (family Bucerotidae) are critical seed dispersers, carrying and dropping large seeds of key forest trees far from the parent plant. In forests where hornbills have been eliminated by hunting and habitat loss, recruitment of large-seeded trees has declined. They are considered "farmers of the forest" and their loss threatens long-term forest regeneration.' },
  { q: 'Which community in Rajasthan is traditionally known for protecting blackbuck and chinkaras to a degree that these animals are virtually tame in their villages?', opts: ['Gujjar community', 'Bishnoi community', 'Meena community', 'Bhil community'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Bishnoi community of Rajasthan has protected wildlife — especially blackbuck and chinkaras — for over 500 years based on the teachings of Guru Jambheshwar (Jambhoji), who forbade his followers from harming animals or cutting green trees. The 1730 Khejarli massacre, where 363 Bishnois died protecting khejri trees, is a defining event in conservation history.' },
  { q: 'What percentage of India\'s geographical area is officially classified as forest as per government records? (Select the option closest to the actual figure)', opts: ['About 12%', 'About 21–23%', 'About 33%', 'About 40%'], ans: 1, exp: 'According to government records and the Forest Survey of India, approximately 21–23% of India\'s geographical area is classified as forest (varying slightly by year and methodology). This is significantly below the National Forest Policy 1988 target of 33%. The discrepancy highlights the gap between policy goals and ground reality in forest conservation.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following correctly describes the difference between "Reserved Forests," "Protected Forests," and "Unclassed Forests"?', opts: ['Reserved Forests allow all human activities; Protected Forests restrict timber harvesting; Unclassed have no restrictions', 'Reserved Forests are most strictly protected (no rights to communities); Protected Forests allow some rights; Unclassed are residual forest lands', 'Unclassed Forests have the highest protection; Reserved Forests allow mining; Protected Forests allow agriculture', 'All three categories have identical management under the Forest Conservation Act 1980'], ans: 1, exp: 'Under Indian forest law: Reserved Forests — no rights of any kind unless specifically granted by the Forest Settlement Officer, most strictly protected. Protected Forests — community rights like grazing and collection may be permitted. Unclassed Forests — residual forest lands owned by government/private parties/communities with varying degrees of protection. About 53% of total forests are Reserved Forests.' },
  { q: 'The loss of forest cover leads to which of the following chain of environmental consequences?', opts: ['Increased rainfall, reduced soil erosion, improved river flow', 'Reduced rainfall interception, increased runoff, soil erosion, river siltation, flooding, and reduced groundwater recharge', 'Lower temperatures near cleared areas, benefiting agriculture', 'Increased oxygen production as grasslands replace forests'], ans: 1, exp: 'Forest loss disrupts the hydrological cycle: without tree canopy, rainfall hits bare soil directly, causing compaction and reduced infiltration. Runoff increases, causing flash floods and soil erosion. Rivers receive more silt (reducing reservoir life and navigability). Groundwater recharge decreases (fewer tree roots to channel water down). Local temperatures increase due to reduced evapotranspiration.' },
  { q: 'The Andaman wild pig (Sus andamanensis) being classified as "Endemic" under NCERT implies that:', opts: ['It is endangered and found in very small numbers globally', 'It is found naturally only in the Andaman Islands and nowhere else in the world', 'It is a domesticated variety of pig unique to the Andaman Islands', 'It is protected under Schedule I of the Wildlife Protection Act 1972 only'], ans: 1, exp: 'Endemic species are those that are naturally confined to a particular geographic region and are not naturally found elsewhere. The Andaman wild pig is endemic to the Andaman Islands. This means its entire global population exists only in those islands, making its survival entirely dependent on the preservation of Andaman forest ecosystems.' },
  { q: 'Chandi Prasad Bhatt, a leader of the Chipko Movement, founded which organisation to channel the movement\'s energy into constructive forest conservation?', opts: ['Navdanya', 'Dasholi Gram Swarajya Mandal (DGSM)', 'Beej Bachao Andolan', 'Tarun Bharat Sangh'], ans: 1, exp: 'Chandi Prasad Bhatt founded the Dasholi Gram Swarajya Mandal (DGSM) in 1964 in Gopeshwar, Chamoli. This cooperative organisation initially focused on small forest-based industries but transformed into the organisational backbone of the Chipko Movement. DGSM channelled community participation into reforestation, eco-development, and sustainable forest use in Uttarakhand.' },
  { q: 'The Navdanya movement, related to forest and biodiversity conservation, was founded by which activist?', opts: ['Gaura Devi', 'Sundarlal Bahuguna', 'Vandana Shiva', 'Rajendra Singh'], ans: 2, exp: 'Navdanya (meaning "nine seeds" or "new gift") was founded by Dr. Vandana Shiva in 1987 as a national movement for seed sovereignty and biodiversity conservation. It has helped set up over 100 community seed banks across India, preserving thousands of traditional crop varieties from corporate monocultures and genetic patenting.' },
  { q: 'India\'s mega-diversity is partly attributed to which geographical factors that have allowed such high species richness?', opts: ['India\'s flat topography enabling species to spread freely across the country', 'India\'s diverse physiography — from Himalayan alpine zones to tropical coasts — creating varied habitats, combined with its position at the junction of Afrotropical, Palearctic, and Indomalayan biogeographical realms', 'India\'s large size alone, with no specific geographic features contributing', 'India\'s desert ecosystems, which support unique drought-adapted species not found elsewhere'], ans: 1, exp: 'India\'s biodiversity is a product of its: (1) Diverse physiography — Himalayan ranges, Deccan plateau, coastal plains, islands; (2) Varied climate — from arctic to tropical; (3) Geological history — India was part of Gondwanaland, then isolated, then connected to Asia, allowing mixing of species from multiple biogeographical realms; (4) Presence of two global biodiversity hotspots: Western Ghats and North-East India.' },
];

// CH15_SOCIAL — Water Resources
const SOCIAL_CH15 = [
  { q: 'What percentage of the world\'s total water is saline (found in oceans and seas)?', opts: ['75%', '85%', '97.5%', '90%'], ans: 2, exp: 'Approximately 97.5% of the world\'s total water is saline water found in oceans and seas, and is not directly usable for drinking, irrigation, or most industrial purposes. Only the remaining 2.5% is fresh water. This scarcity of freshwater makes its management critically important for human survival.' },
  { q: 'Of the world\'s total freshwater (2.5%), approximately what fraction is locked in polar ice caps and glaciers and thus unavailable for immediate use?', opts: ['30%', '50%', '70%', '90%'], ans: 2, exp: 'About 70% of the world\'s freshwater is stored in polar ice caps, glaciers, and permanent snow cover, making it unavailable for human use. Of the remaining freshwater, most is groundwater, with only about 0.4% available as surface water and atmospheric water — the sources most accessible to humans.' },
  { q: 'What percentage of the world\'s surface water and atmospheric water — the most accessible form of freshwater — is accounted for by the total available?', opts: ['0.04%', '0.4%', '2%', '5%'], ans: 1, exp: 'Only about 0.4% of the world\'s total water exists as surface water (rivers, lakes, wetlands) and atmospheric water. This is the water most readily accessible for human use. The extreme scarcity of this resource — a tiny fraction of total water — underscores why water conservation and efficient use are critical global imperatives.' },
  { q: 'India receives approximately what share of global precipitation, yet holds what share of the world\'s population?', opts: ['4% of precipitation, 16% of population', '8% of precipitation, 16% of population', '4% of precipitation, 20% of population', '16% of precipitation, 4% of population'], ans: 0, exp: 'India receives about 4% of the world\'s precipitation but is home to about 16% of the world\'s population. This imbalance — receiving far less water relative to its population — explains why water stress is a persistent challenge in India and why per capita freshwater availability is so much lower than the global average.' },
  { q: 'India\'s per capita annual freshwater availability declined from approximately 6,008 cubic metres in 1951 to approximately 1,820 cubic metres in 2001. This decline is primarily due to:', opts: ['Reduced total rainfall over India in the 20th century', 'Rapid population growth which divides the same water among more people', 'Depletion of glacier-fed river systems', 'Diversion of Indian rivers to neighbouring countries'], ans: 1, exp: 'The sharp decline in per capita water availability from 6,008 m³ (1951) to 1,820 m³ (2001) is NOT because total water has decreased significantly — India\'s total annual precipitation has remained roughly stable. The primary cause is population growth: India\'s population roughly tripled over this period, dividing the same water resource among far more people.' },
  { q: 'The Bhakra-Nangal Dam project is built on which rivers, and what is the name of its reservoir?', opts: ['River Ravi — Ranjit Sagar Reservoir', 'Sutlej and Beas rivers — Gobind Sagar Reservoir', 'River Beas — Pong Reservoir', 'River Yamuna — Renuka Reservoir'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Bhakra-Nangal Dam is built across the Sutlej River at Bhakra (with the Nangal barrage downstream) in Himachal Pradesh/Punjab. The reservoir formed is called Gobind Sagar (named after Guru Gobind Singh). It was inaugurated in 1963 and is one of India\'s highest gravity dams at 226 metres. Jawaharlal Nehru called multipurpose projects "temples of modern India."' },
  { q: 'Jawaharlal Nehru\'s famous phrase "temples of modern India" referred to which type of projects, and what did he mean by this metaphor?', opts: ['Nuclear power plants — symbols of India\'s technological future', 'Multipurpose river valley projects — symbols of development, national integration, and India\'s ability to harness nature for progress', 'Industrial factories — representing economic modernity', 'Universities — representing knowledge as the new religion'], ans: 1, exp: 'Nehru called multipurpose river valley projects (dams) "temples of modern India" to convey that they were the new sacred sites of a developing nation — they generated electricity, provided irrigation, controlled floods, and promoted national integration. However, critics argue this romanticised vision ignored the social costs of massive displacement and ecological damage.' },
  { q: 'The Hirakud Dam on the Mahanadi River holds which notable record?', opts: ['India\'s tallest dam', 'World\'s longest earthen dam', 'India\'s first underground dam', 'World\'s largest reservoir by area'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Hirakud Dam in Odisha, built across the Mahanadi River, is the world\'s longest earthen dam at about 26 km (the main dam is 4.8 km; with dykes it stretches 26 km). Completed in 1957, it was one of India\'s first major post-independence multipurpose projects, providing irrigation, flood control, and hydroelectric power.' },
  { q: 'The Sardar Sarovar Project (SSP) on the Narmada River became controversial primarily because of which issue?', opts: ['The dam was found to be structurally unsound after construction', 'Massive displacement of people — primarily tribal communities — without adequate rehabilitation, and ecological damage', 'The dam flooded historical temples and archaeological sites', 'Cost overruns that made electricity generation economically unviable'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Sardar Sarovar Project sparked the Narmada Bachao Andolan because the dam\'s reservoir was estimated to displace between 200,000 and 320,000 people, predominantly Adivasi (tribal) communities in MP, Maharashtra, and Gujarat. Critics argued rehabilitation was inadequate and the dam\'s benefits (primarily irrigation) would accrue to large farmers in Gujarat while costs were borne by poor tribals in Madhya Pradesh.' },
  { q: 'The Narmada Bachao Andolan (NBA), which opposed the Sardar Sarovar Dam, was led by which activist?', opts: ['Vandana Shiva', 'Medha Patkar', 'Rajendra Singh', 'Aruna Roy'], ans: 1, exp: 'Medha Patkar is the primary leader and founder of the Narmada Bachao Andolan (Save the Narmada Movement), formed in 1985. The NBA brought together tribals, farmers, and environmentalists to challenge the Sardar Sarovar Project in the courts and through direct action (long marches, hunger strikes, threatening to drown rather than move). The case reached the Supreme Court of India.' },
  { q: 'An estimated how many people have been displaced by large dams and other development projects in India since independence?', opts: ['10 million', '25 million', '40 million', '60 million'], ans: 2, exp: 'According to estimates cited by the NCERT and other sources, approximately 40 million people (4 crore) have been displaced by large dams and similar projects since independence in 1947. A disproportionate share of the displaced are tribal and lower caste communities — people who are least able to negotiate fair compensation and who often lose cultural and livelihood connections to land.' },
  { q: 'The Nagarjuna Sagar Dam is built on which river and primarily serves which states?', opts: ['Tungabhadra — Karnataka and Andhra Pradesh', 'Godavari — Telangana and Andhra Pradesh', 'Krishna — Telangana and Andhra Pradesh', 'Cauvery — Karnataka and Tamil Nadu'], ans: 2, exp: 'Nagarjuna Sagar Dam is built across the Krishna River between Nalgonda (Telangana) and Guntur (Andhra Pradesh) districts. Completed in 1969, it is one of the largest masonry dams in the world. It provides irrigation water to millions of acres in the Krishna River delta region of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana.' },
  { q: 'The Indira Gandhi Canal (formerly Rajasthan Canal), which transformed the Thar Desert through irrigation, begins at which location?', opts: ['Bhakra Dam on the Sutlej River', 'Harike Barrage at the confluence of Sutlej and Beas rivers in Punjab', 'Nangal Dam in Himachal Pradesh', 'Bhakra-Nangal Dam, dividing waters directly'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Indira Gandhi Canal starts at Harike Barrage, located at the confluence of the Sutlej and Beas rivers in Punjab. The main canal is 445 km long, running through Rajasthan to Barmer district. It has transformed arid Rajasthan, enabling cultivation of previously barren desert land and supporting drinking water supply in the Thar region.' },
  { q: 'The introduction of the Indira Gandhi Canal into the Thar Desert region also created which unintended negative ecological consequence?', opts: ['Desertification of previously irrigated areas', 'Waterlogging and soil salinity (salinisation) in canal-irrigated areas', 'Depletion of the Indus River\'s flow in Pakistan', 'Reduction in migratory bird populations in Rajasthan'], ans: 1, exp: 'While the Indira Gandhi Canal brought life to the Thar Desert, it also caused waterlogging and soil salinisation in some areas due to excessive irrigation without adequate drainage. When canal water saturates soil and evaporates, it leaves behind dissolved salts, rendering the soil increasingly infertile. This is a common problem with large-scale irrigation projects in arid regions.' },
  { q: 'The traditional water harvesting structure called "tanka" found in Rajasthan is best described as:', opts: ['A large earthen check dam built across seasonal streams', 'A small underground cylindrical cistern (typically about 8 feet × 3 feet) built in houses to collect rooftop rainwater', 'A communal tank fed by the monsoon, shared by an entire village', 'A subsurface sand dam that retains water in sandy riverbeds'], ans: 1, exp: 'A tanka (plural: tankis) is a small underground cylindrical rainwater harvesting structure, typically about 2.4m (8 ft) in diameter and 0.9m (3 ft) deep, built in the courtyard or underground near houses in Rajasthan. Rooftop channels direct rainwater into the tanka. It stores enough water for a family through the dry season and protects water from evaporation and contamination.' },
  { q: 'The "khadin" system of water harvesting, found in Jaisalmer, Rajasthan, operates on which principle?', opts: ['Drawing groundwater through traditional rope-and-pulley well systems', 'Harvesting runoff from slopes by constructing earthen embankments (dhoras) that impound water and allow silt to settle, then farming the moist silt', 'Diverting water from perennial rivers through underground stone channels', 'Building tiered terraces on hillsides to slow runoff and increase infiltration'], ans: 1, exp: 'Khadins (called "dhoras" locally) are traditional agrohydrological systems from the Jaisalmer region. An earthen embankment (dhora) is built at the base of a long sloping upland catchment. Runoff collects behind the embankment, depositing silt. When water drains away or evaporates, the moist, silt-enriched land behind the bund is cultivated — a brilliant system of combining water and soil conservation.' },
  { q: 'Johads are traditional water harvesting structures associated primarily with which state and which modern revivalist?', opts: ['Maharashtra — Rajendar Singh', 'Rajasthan — Rajendra Singh (the "waterman of India")', 'Karnataka — Dr. G.N.S. Reddy', 'Andhra Pradesh — P.C. Pragada'], ans: 1, exp: 'Johads are traditional earthen check dams or ponds in Rajasthan that harvest rainwater. Rajendra Singh (Magsaysay Award winner 2001, called the "waterman of India") revived johad construction in Alwar district through the Tarun Bharat Sangh (TBS) NGO. The revival of johads helped recharge groundwater, restore five rivers, and bring back wildlife — a landmark example of community-based water management.' },
  { q: 'The Tarun Bharat Sangh (TBS), which revived traditional johad water harvesting structures in Rajasthan, was founded by:', opts: ['Medha Patkar', 'Rajendra Singh', 'Anna Hazare', 'P.V. Rajagopalan'], ans: 1, exp: 'Tarun Bharat Sangh was founded by Rajendra Singh in 1975 and became famous for its community-based revival of johads in Alwar district, Rajasthan, from the 1980s onwards. TBS built thousands of johads, which recharged groundwater so effectively that five rivers — including the Arvari — returned to near-perennial flow. Rajendra Singh won the Stockholm Water Prize in 2015.' },
  { q: 'The bamboo drip irrigation system of Meghalaya has been practised for approximately how long and involves which engineering principle?', opts: ['50 years — using electric pumps to circulate water through bamboo pipes', '200 years — using gravity to channel water from streams through a network of bamboo pipes and channels to fields', '100 years — using tidal energy to push water uphill through bamboo conduits', '500 years — involving underground bamboo aqueducts built by ancient kingdoms'], ans: 1, exp: 'The bamboo drip irrigation system of Meghalaya is estimated to be about 200 years old. Farmers in the Khasi and Jaintia hills construct intricate networks of bamboo pipes of varying diameters that channel water from streams and springs, using gravity, across long distances to terraced garden plots. The system delivers water in controlled drips — an indigenous form of precision irrigation.' },
  { q: 'The traditional irrigation channels called "kuls" (also "kulhs" in Himachal Pradesh) are primarily found in which geographical zone and are fed by:', opts: ['Coastal Tamil Nadu — fed by monsoon-season rivers', 'Western Himalayan valleys — diversion channels fed by glacial meltwater and mountain streams', 'Deccan Plateau — fed by dug wells tapping deep aquifers', 'Rajasthan\'s Thar Desert — fed by groundwater springs'], ans: 1, exp: 'Kuls (kulhs) are traditional irrigation channels in the western Himalayas, particularly in Himachal Pradesh. They are constructed by diverting water from glacial streams and mountain rivulets into channels that run along hill contours to terraced fields below. The system is community-managed with elaborate rules about water sharing and maintenance of the channels.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is the most accurate explanation for why large multipurpose dams often fail to deliver projected irrigation benefits?', opts: ['Engineers consistently miscalculate dam heights', 'Sedimentation of reservoirs reduces storage capacity; waterlogging and salinisation in canal-command areas reduce farm productivity; and social conflicts over water distribution undermine efficiency', 'Dams are too far from agricultural fields to transport water efficiently', 'Dam turbines generate electricity at the expense of irrigation water'], ans: 1, exp: 'Large dams routinely underperform irrigation projections because: (1) Reservoirs silt up faster than predicted, reducing storage; (2) Waterlogging and salinisation in canal-irrigated areas reduces farmland productivity; (3) Political and inter-state conflicts over water allocation impede distribution; (4) Lack of equitable water user associations leads to irrigation benefiting powerful farmers near the canal head, leaving tail-end farmers dry.' },
  { q: 'The Cauvery Water Dispute between Karnataka and Tamil Nadu is an example of which broader problem discussed in the water resources chapter?', opts: ['Insufficient total river flow due to climate change', 'Inter-state water conflicts arising from competition over shared river resources', 'Central government mismanagement of river basin authorities', 'Pollution of shared rivers preventing use by downstream states'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Cauvery dispute (Karnataka and Tamil Nadu also involving Kerala and Puducherry) exemplifies inter-state water conflicts in India. Karnataka constructs dams and diverts water for irrigation; Tamil Nadu, the downstream state, claims this reduces its share. Despite the Cauvery Water Disputes Tribunal (1990, award in 2007) and Supreme Court orders, conflicts persist — illustrating that water is increasingly a source of political and social conflict.' },
  { q: 'Rooftop rainwater harvesting is now legally mandated in many Indian cities. Which city became a notable early example of making rooftop harvesting compulsory for new buildings?', opts: ['Mumbai', 'Chennai', 'Delhi', 'Bengaluru'], ans: 1, exp: 'Chennai was one of the first Indian cities to make rooftop rainwater harvesting mandatory for all buildings (2003). The move was driven by severe groundwater depletion and water scarcity. The programme significantly increased groundwater recharge levels in the city within a few years. Tamil Nadu as a whole enacted legislation requiring rainwater harvesting structures in all buildings.' },
  { q: 'The disruption of river flow by large dams primarily damages aquatic ecosystems by:', opts: ['Increasing water temperature in upstream sections', 'Blocking fish migration, altering sediment flow downstream, changing seasonal flood pulses that ecosystems depend upon, and degrading downstream floodplain ecosystems', 'Making river water too clear — removing nutrients that fish depend on', 'Preventing monsoon rainfall from reaching river catchments'], ans: 1, exp: 'Dams disrupt rivers as ecological corridors: (1) They block migratory fish (like hilsa in Ganga-Brahmaputra) from reaching spawning grounds; (2) Trap sediment behind the dam, starving downstream floodplains and deltas; (3) Eliminate natural flood pulses that seasonally inundate floodplains — critical for wetland ecosystems; (4) Reduce downstream river temperature fluctuations; (5) Create reservoirs that emit methane from decomposing submerged vegetation.' },
  { q: 'The Ganga Action Plan (GAP), launched in 1985 to clean the Ganga, is relevant to water resources because it illustrates which challenge?', opts: ['The difficulty of dam construction on large rivers', 'The gap between policy intent and implementation — despite massive investment, pollution levels remained high due to inadequate sewage treatment, industrial discharge, and poor monitoring', 'The success of centralised water management by the Indian government', 'The effectiveness of multipurpose projects in improving river water quality'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Ganga Action Plan (Phase I: 1985, Phase II: 1993) invested thousands of crores in sewage treatment plants and ghats, yet the Ganga remains heavily polluted. This illustrates that: (1) Point-source treatment alone cannot address diffuse agricultural and urban runoff; (2) Many treatment plants were dysfunctional; (3) Industrial effluents bypassed regulation; (4) Population growth outpaced infrastructure — a cautionary tale for water policy.' },
  { q: 'The water scarcity experienced in cities like Chennai, Bengaluru, and Hyderabad despite being in regions with reasonable rainfall is primarily caused by which factor?', opts: ['These cities receive no rainfall at all', 'Over-dependence on surface water; failure to harvest rainwater; destruction of local water bodies; and rapid urban growth outpacing water supply infrastructure', 'Rivers being diverted away from South India to North India', 'Domestic water consumption being much higher than industrial use'], ans: 1, exp: 'Urban water scarcity in South Indian cities results from: (1) Destruction of traditional lakes and tanks due to urbanisation; (2) Failure to harvest the significant but seasonal rainfall they receive; (3) Over-extraction of groundwater causing depletion; (4) Rapid population growth outpacing water supply; (5) Inefficient distribution with 30–50% losses in leaky pipes. The problem is primarily governance and infrastructure, not rainfall deficit.' },
  { q: 'The sedimentation problem in large reservoirs refers to:', opts: ['The buildup of salt crystals on reservoir walls', 'The accumulation of silt and sediment behind the dam, progressively reducing reservoir storage capacity and eventually rendering it ineffective', 'The deposition of chemical waste from industrial effluents in the reservoir', 'The growth of aquatic weeds that block reservoir inlets'], ans: 1, exp: 'Rivers carry large quantities of suspended sediment (silt, clay, sand). When this sediment-laden water enters a reservoir, it slows and deposits its sediment load. Over decades, this sediment accumulates, reducing the reservoir\'s "live storage" capacity. For example, the Gobind Sagar reservoir (Bhakra) has lost a significant percentage of its original capacity to sedimentation since 1963. This is a fundamental, irreversible problem with large dams.' },
  { q: 'The "multiplier effect" of multipurpose river projects refers to which concept?', opts: ['The fact that one dam can generate electricity multiplied by the number of turbines', 'The idea that benefits like irrigation, power generation, flood control, navigation, and recreation compound from a single project', 'The financial return that is a multiple of the original investment cost', 'The geometric increase in water availability as more tributaries are dammed'], ans: 1, exp: 'The "multiplier effect" of multipurpose projects means a single dam serves multiple simultaneous purposes: irrigation (transforms agriculture), electricity generation (fuels industrialisation), flood control (protects downstream cities), navigation improvement, domestic/industrial water supply, and fisheries/recreation. Nehru\'s "temple" metaphor captured this vision of one project triggering cascading development benefits.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "watershed management" differs from building large dams in that it focuses on:', opts: ['Constructing large reservoirs at river mouths', 'Small-scale, decentralised interventions at the catchment level — bunding, check dams, contour trenching, afforestation — to maximise local water retention and groundwater recharge', 'Diverting river water from water-surplus to water-deficit regions through inter-basin transfer', 'Managing water pricing and distribution through market mechanisms'], ans: 1, exp: 'Watershed management is a decentralised, bottom-up approach that works at the village and small-catchment level. It involves: contour bunding to slow runoff; small check dams to harvest rainwater; afforestation of degraded slopes to increase infiltration; and village-level governance of water resources. This approach, exemplified by the Tarun Bharat Sangh\'s johad revival, is environmentally sustainable and socially equitable compared to large dams.' },
  { q: 'The Krishna-Godavari water dispute involves which states, and what type of conflict does it represent?', opts: ['Gujarat and Rajasthan — groundwater extraction dispute', 'Andhra Pradesh/Telangana and Maharashtra/Karnataka — conflict over upper-riparian states\'s dam construction affecting downstream water flows', 'Odisha and Chhattisgarh — dispute over Mahanadi River', 'Tamil Nadu and Kerala — dispute over Periyar River'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Krishna-Godavari water dispute primarily involves Andhra Pradesh (and Telangana), Maharashtra, and Karnataka. Upper-riparian states (Maharashtra, Karnataka) build dams and diversions on the Krishna and Godavari rivers, reducing downstream flows to Andhra Pradesh/Telangana. Similar dynamics underlie many Indian inter-state water conflicts, where upstream and downstream interests fundamentally clash.' },
  { q: 'Traditional stepwells (vav/baoli), found extensively in Gujarat and Rajasthan, represent which type of water management technology?', opts: ['Large-scale surface water storage for irrigation', 'Sophisticated architectural groundwater access structures that also provided cool public spaces and social gathering points', 'Underground aqueducts connecting distant water sources to towns', 'Filtration systems for purifying river water before distribution'], ans: 1, exp: 'Stepwells (vav in Gujarat, baoli in Rajasthan/Delhi) are elaborate underground structures with descending flights of stairs leading to a well, allowing year-round access to groundwater that fluctuates seasonally. They were engineering marvels — Rani ki Vav in Patan, Gujarat (UNESCO World Heritage) demonstrates both technical sophistication and artistic refinement. They served drinking, ritual, and social purposes.' },
  { q: 'Excessive groundwater extraction for irrigation, as seen in Punjab, Haryana, and Andhra Pradesh, creates which long-term problem?', opts: ['Excessive flooding of fields as groundwater rises above surface level', 'Falling water tables (aquifer depletion), land subsidence, increased pumping costs, and eventual failure of tube wells — threatening agricultural sustainability', 'Saline intrusion into freshwater aquifers only in coastal areas', 'Increased river flow as excess groundwater drains into rivers'], ans: 1, exp: 'Overextraction of groundwater (where extraction exceeds recharge) causes: (1) Falling water tables — tube wells must be deepened at escalating cost; (2) Land subsidence as aquifer material compacts; (3) In coastal areas, saline seawater intrudes into depleted freshwater aquifers (e.g., coastal Saurashtra, coastal Andhra Pradesh); (4) In some areas (like Punjab\'s Green Revolution belt), water tables have fallen several metres per decade, threatening long-term food security.' },
  { q: 'The river linking (interlinking of rivers) project proposed in India aims to transfer water from "surplus" to "deficit" river basins. The primary criticism of this project is:', opts: ['The technology for large-scale water transfer does not yet exist', 'Massive ecological disruption, huge displacement, disruption of coastal ecosystems by reduced river outflow, inter-state political conflicts, and astronomical costs — with disputed assumptions about which rivers are truly "surplus"', 'It would not provide enough water to make a difference', 'All Indian rivers are already overused, so no surplus water exists anywhere'], ans: 1, exp: 'Critics of the National River Linking Project (NRLP) argue: (1) "Surplus" river designation is scientifically contested — rivers need their full flow to maintain deltas, estuaries, and mangroves; (2) Thousands of km of canals would require massive land acquisition and displacement; (3) Inter-state conflicts (as seen with existing water disputes) would multiply; (4) Costs are enormous; (5) Ecological disruption to donor basins would be severe.' },
  { q: 'The "water user associations" concept, promoted as part of irrigation reform, aims to address which specific failure of large dam projects?', opts: ['The failure of dams to generate sufficient electricity', 'The inequitable distribution of irrigation water — where head-end (canal-top) farmers get excess water while tail-end farmers receive none — by giving users democratic management control', 'The failure to construct canals wide enough to carry dam water', 'The tendency of large dams to cause earthquakes in seismically active regions'], ans: 1, exp: 'A classic problem with large canal systems is "head-end bias": farmers near the canal head receive abundant water (often over-irrigating), while tail-end farmers get little or none. Water User Associations (WUAs) — elected bodies of farmers — take over day-to-day management and maintenance of distributary canals, theoretically ensuring equitable distribution, cost recovery for maintenance, and farmer accountability.' },
  { q: 'Large dam projects near seismically active zones carry which specific additional risk?', opts: ['The pressure of water in large reservoirs can trigger induced seismicity — earthquakes caused by the weight of water and lubrication of fault lines', 'Dams in earthquake zones are built with substandard materials', 'Earthquakes always occur before dam construction is complete, preventing completion', 'The vibration of dam turbines amplifies natural seismic waves'], ans: 0, exp: 'Reservoir-induced seismicity (RIS) is a documented phenomenon where the weight of water in large reservoirs increases stress on existing fault lines and the water lubricates fault surfaces, potentially triggering earthquakes. The Koyna earthquake (1967, Maharashtra, magnitude 6.3 — India\'s deadliest reservoir-induced earthquake) occurred near the Koyna Dam and is the most cited Indian example. The Sardar Sarovar project area also has seismic concerns.' },
  { q: 'The famous Periyar Dam (1895) in Kerala was built during the British period and involved an unusual arrangement whereby:', opts: ['The British diverted the westward-flowing Periyar River through a tunnel to irrigate the Madurai region in Tamil Nadu — across a watershed divide', 'The British built the dam to provide drinking water exclusively to colonial cities', 'The dam was built as a joint venture between the Travancore state and the British East India Company', 'The dam was designed to generate electricity for the British navy in Kochi'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Mullaperiyar Dam (technically Mullaperiyar, but commonly referred to as Periyar Dam) was built in 1895 by the British in Travancore (now Kerala) to divert the westward-flowing Periyar River through a tunnel in the Western Ghats, eastward into the dry Madurai and Dindigul districts of Tamil Nadu. This inter-basin transfer — still operating today — is at the centre of an ongoing Kerala-Tamil Nadu water dispute over the dam\'s structural safety.' },
  { q: 'The decline in per capita freshwater availability in India is expected to continue in coming decades primarily because:', opts: ['Indian rivers are drying up due to reduced Himalayan snowfall', 'Population is still growing, increasing demand, while pollution and overextraction are reducing the quality and availability of usable water', 'India is transferring freshwater to other countries through international treaties', 'Global warming is eliminating all monsoon rainfall over South Asia'], ans: 1, exp: 'Per capita water availability will likely decline further because: (1) Population growth (India became the world\'s most populous country in 2023) continues to divide the same resource among more people; (2) Groundwater depletion reduces the buffer of stored water; (3) Pollution makes available water unusable; (4) Climate change is making rainfall more erratic; (5) Agricultural demand keeps growing with food security needs.' },
  { q: 'The Bhakra-Nangal Dam was inaugurated in 1963 by which Indian Prime Minister, and what was the occasion\'s historical significance?', opts: ['Nehru, marking India\'s first hydroelectric project', 'Nehru, dedicating one of India\'s largest irrigation-cum-power projects and symbolising post-independence development ambition', 'Lal Bahadur Shastri, inaugurating the first dam to end the Bengal famine', 'Indira Gandhi, marking the nationalisation of river waters'], ans: 1, exp: 'Bhakra-Nangal was dedicated to the nation by Jawaharlal Nehru in 1963 — though construction had been ongoing since the 1940s-50s. It symbolised India\'s post-independence developmental aspiration — harnessing the Sutlej River to irrigate Punjab and Haryana (contributing to the Green Revolution) and generate electricity for northern India. It remains one of India\'s most important multipurpose dam projects.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "virtual water" is relevant to water resources because it refers to:', opts: ['Water stored underground in virtual (invisible) aquifers', 'The water embedded in agricultural products — exporting water-intensive crops (like rice, sugarcane) is equivalent to exporting water itself, potentially worsening water scarcity in exporting countries', 'Computer simulations used to model water flow in river systems', 'Water desalinated from the sea that is technically available but not practically usable'], ans: 1, exp: 'Virtual water is the hidden water embedded in agricultural and industrial products. When India exports rice (requiring 3,000–5,000 litres/kg) or sugarcane products, it effectively exports this water. For a water-stressed country like India, exporting water-intensive crops to wealthy countries while its own farmers face water scarcity represents a resource governance challenge, though this concept extends beyond the NCERT textbook level.' },
  { q: 'The water conflict in the Cauvery basin is complicated by which structural factor?', opts: ['The Cauvery River flows from Tamil Nadu into Karnataka, making Tamil Nadu the upper-riparian state', 'Karnataka is the upper-riparian state (controlling dam construction at Krishnaraja Sagara and Kabini), while Tamil Nadu depends on the river for drinking water and paddy cultivation in the delta — creating a classic upstream-downstream conflict', 'The Cauvery is an international river shared with Sri Lanka', 'The conflict is about groundwater, not surface water sharing'], ans: 1, exp: 'Karnataka (upper-riparian) has built reservoirs like Krishnaraja Sagara (KRS) Dam that regulate Cauvery flow into Tamil Nadu (lower-riparian). Tamil Nadu\'s Cauvery delta farmers and Chennai\'s water supply depend on this flow. In drought years, both states claim the same limited water. The Cauvery Water Management Authority (2018) now allocates monthly releases, but tensions persist, especially in drought years.' },
  { q: 'The Integrated Water Resource Management (IWRM) approach, as implied by the chapter\'s discussion of traditional systems, advocates for:', opts: ['Maximum dam construction to store all available rainfall', 'A holistic approach that combines surface water, groundwater, rainwater harvesting, demand management, and watershed protection — with community participation in governance', 'Privatisation of all water resources to improve efficiency', 'Dependence exclusively on large-scale engineering solutions'], ans: 1, exp: 'IWRM recognises that water management must integrate: surface water and groundwater (as a connected system); supply-side (building storage) and demand-side (reducing waste); large engineering and traditional small-scale systems; upstream watershed health and downstream users\' needs; and formal state management with community participation. The contrast between large dam failures and johad successes in the chapter implicitly makes this case.' },
  { q: 'In which region of India has rooftop rainwater harvesting been practised for centuries to collect water into underground tanks called "tankas"?', opts: ['The Kerala coast', 'The Bihar plains', 'The Rajasthan desert region', 'The Assam hills'], ans: 2, exp: 'Tankas (underground cylindrical cisterns) are a centuries-old rainwater harvesting tradition in Rajasthan, where annual rainfall is low (100–400 mm) and groundwater is often brackish. Every household traditionally had a tanka in the courtyard. Rainwater from the roof was channelled through pipes into the tanka. The first rain washed the roof; subsequent rains were stored. The tanka kept water cool and protected from evaporation.' },
  { q: 'The specific figure for India\'s per capita annual freshwater availability in 1951 was approximately 6,008 m³. This figure declined to 1,820 m³ by 2001. What does this statistic specifically measure?', opts: ['The total water used per person for all purposes', 'The total naturally available freshwater divided by the population — a measure of the theoretical freshwater availability per person', 'The actual water delivered to households by government water utilities', 'The irrigated water used per acre of farmland'], ans: 1, exp: 'Per capita freshwater availability (or per capita internal renewable freshwater resources) is calculated by dividing a country\'s total annual renewable freshwater (precipitation-fed rivers and groundwater recharge) by its population. It is a theoretical measure of how much water nature makes available per person — not how much people actually access. The decline from 6,008 m³ to 1,820 m³ reflects population growth alone, not reduced rainfall.' },
  { q: 'The social impacts of large dams include which of the following NOT mentioned in the NCERT as a concern?', opts: ['Displacement of communities from reservoir areas', 'Loss of livelihoods for people dependent on forests and farms in submerged areas', 'Spread of vector-borne diseases (malaria, etc.) in reservoir areas', 'Increase in agricultural productivity for all displaced communities immediately after resettlement'], ans: 3, exp: 'The NCERT highlights dam-related displacement, loss of livelihoods (farming, fishing, forest-dependent livelihoods), cultural alienation, and inadequate rehabilitation. Spread of vector-borne diseases (malaria, Japanese encephalitis) in stagnant reservoir water is also a documented problem. However, displaced communities typically face severe impoverishment — agricultural productivity does NOT immediately increase for them; resettlement lands are usually marginal.' },
  { q: 'The Kulhs (irrigation channels) of Himachal Pradesh are significant because they demonstrate which principle of traditional water management?', opts: ['Community-designed and community-managed water systems that pre-date government irrigation by centuries and effectively distribute glacial meltwater to multiple villages', 'Systems designed and maintained exclusively by the state government', 'Underground aqueducts bringing water from Himalayan rivers to the Indo-Gangetic plains', 'Reservoirs built by local kings to store monsoon water for summer use'], ans: 0, exp: 'Kulhs (kulhs) are irrigation channels in Himachal Pradesh\'s Spiti and other valleys that divert water from glacial streams and mountain springs to terraced fields. They are community property, maintained by collective labour according to traditional rules. Water is shared equitably among villages along the channel. The system functioned for hundreds of years before government irrigation arrived — demonstrating effective indigenous water governance.' },
  { q: 'India\'s 4% share of global precipitation is unevenly distributed. Which statement best describes this internal distribution challenge?', opts: ['Most rainfall is evenly distributed across India throughout the year', 'About 75% of India\'s rainfall falls in just 4 months (June–September) and is concentrated in certain regions, creating extreme temporal and spatial mismatch between water availability and demand', 'North India receives 90% of rainfall; South India is virtually rainless', 'Rainfall is highest in Rajasthan and lowest in Kerala'], ans: 1, exp: 'India\'s rainfall distribution is highly uneven: (1) Temporal — about 75% falls during the 4-month southwest monsoon (June–Sept), requiring storage for the other 8 months; (2) Spatial — Northeast India receives over 1,000 cm/year while Rajasthan receives under 10 cm; (3) Inter-annual variability — drought and flood years alternate unpredictably. This mismatch between water availability and demand is the fundamental challenge of Indian water management.' },
  { q: 'The Bengal famine of 1943, though primarily a policy failure, illustrated water resource dependency because:', opts: ['The famine was caused by a catastrophic drought that dried all rivers in Bengal', 'The rice-based food economy of Bengal was highly dependent on monsoon-fed irrigation; any disruption to the monsoon could amplify food insecurity in the absence of adequate storage and distribution infrastructure', 'The British deliberately drained Bengal\'s rivers to cause famine', 'Floods from the Brahmaputra River destroyed all crops in Bengal that year'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Bengal Famine (1943) — while primarily caused by wartime policies, market failures, and deliberate hoarding — was exacerbated by the vulnerability of a rice economy entirely dependent on monsoon-fed water. The cyclone and flooding of 1942-43 damaged crops. The lack of water storage infrastructure and the monsoon-dependence of paddy cultivation meant that climate disruptions could rapidly translate into food crisis — a lesson about the importance of water resilience.' },
  { q: 'Which ancient Indian text contains detailed guidelines on water harvesting, irrigation, and tank construction — indicating that water management has been central to Indian civilisation for millennia?', opts: ['Arthashastra (Kautilya, circa 4th century BCE)', 'Mahabharata', 'Manusmriti', 'Ramayana'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Arthashastra, attributed to Kautilya (Chanakya), contains detailed sections on the construction and maintenance of reservoirs, irrigation tanks, and channels. It specifies tax concessions for farmers who construct irrigation works and penalties for damaging water infrastructure. This confirms that sophisticated water management — including both large state works and small private tanks — was central to Mauryan political economy and Indian civilisation from ancient times.' },
];
